      <p class="byline">Lithium · Last reviewed 2026-11-05</p>
      <h1>Marine Lithium Conversion — Charging, Isolation and Insurance</h1>
      <div class="takeaways"><strong>Quick answer:</strong> A marine lithium conversion is a charging-system project, not a battery swap. The alternator, shore charger, solar controller and BMS must agree on voltage and current; the installation must be isolated, fused and inspected; and the insurer must know.</div>

<h2>Why the charging system decides the project</h2>
<p>A lithium pack accepts charge quickly and holds a higher voltage than lead-acid. A standard alternator or shore charger may overheat trying to feed it, or may stop at a voltage that leaves the pack partial. The charger must be lithium-compatible, or have a profile the BMS can control.</p>
<table><thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>What changes</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Alternator</td><td>May need a regulator or a DC-DC charger; direct alternator charging can overheat</td></tr>
<tr><td>Shore charger</td><td>Lithium profile, correct absorption and float voltages</td></tr>
<tr><td>Solar controller</td><td>Lithium profile and temperature compensation off</td></tr>
<tr><td>BMS</td><td>Controls charge/discharge, balances cells, reports to the system</td></tr>
<tr><td>Isolation</td><td>DC system isolated from the hull; fuses and disconnects rated for the current</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<h2>Isolation and safety</h2>
<p>Marine installations add water, salt and vibration. Use tinned cable, sealed terminals, fuses at the battery, and a disconnect that can break the full current. Keep the pack out of the bilge and away from fuel vapour. A lithium fire on a boat is a different problem from a lead-acid spill; the installation must be designed so that a venting cell is not trapped.</p>
<h2>Standards and insurance</h2>
<p>ABYC E-13 and the applicable ISO/CE requirements cover lithium installation in recreational craft. The insurer will ask who installed the system and whether the components are listed for marine use. A DIY conversion may be technically sound and still uninsurable; confirm before buying the pack.</p>
<h2>A conversion checklist</h2>
<ol><li>Audit the loads and the charging sources.</li><li>Choose a pack voltage and BMS that match the existing system.</li><li>Confirm charger and alternator compatibility in writing.</li><li>Design fusing, isolation and ventilation.</li><li>Get the installation inspected and notify the insurer.</li></ol>

